// Fixture for a `[bool]` field in the FlatBuffers codec (driftsys/ridl#518),
// read by flatbuffers_bool_vector.rs.
//
// `flatbuffers_roundtrip.ridl`'s only boolean field, `engaged` (of the named
// type `Engaged`), is an inline scalar, not a vector, so the codec's
// inline-scalar encode path (`Field::Bool(value)`) is the only one it
// exercises. A vector element is encoded on a different path, one that
// writes every scalar element's raw bytes with `.to_le_bytes()` — a method
// `bool` does not have. This file isolates that path for both shapes a
// boolean vector element can take: `values` is a vector of the bare
// `boolean` primitive, and `engaged` is a vector of `Engaged`, a named
// scalar over the same backing (typl's vacuous-constraint case, Epic 10 Task
// 4). The two differ in the codec's `Scalar::repr` but share its
// `Scalar::prim`, which is what the fix keys on.
//
// Kept self-contained, in the shape of `flatbuffers_roundtrip.ridl`: no
// cross-package import, one declaration carrying both shapes under test.

package fb.boolvec

/// A boolean scalar: its constraint checks nothing, so its constructor is
/// the infallible `new` (Epic 10 Task 4), the same as
/// `flatbuffers_roundtrip.ridl`'s `Engaged`.
type Engaged : boolean

struct Flags {
  values  : [boolean; 0..4]
  engaged : [Engaged; 0..4]
}
